# Forbidden patterns for Go files (*.go). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: go
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Case-sensitive ERE (grep -anE). Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/go.txt`.
# Active rules flag leftover debug output plus one security sink (TLS bypass);
# panic/log.Fatal live in optional below.

(^|[^A-Za-z_.])fmt\.Print(ln|f)?[[:space:]]*\(	Remove fmt.Print/Println/Printf debug output - use the project logger
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])println[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin println() before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])spew\.Dump[[:space:]]*\(	Remove spew.Dump() debug dump before committing

# --- Security: TLS bypass ---
# `go vet` does not catch this and the golangci-lint CI job ships commented out,
# so nothing else in the scaffold sees it.
#
# TLS: the Go twin of backend.txt's `verify=False` and frontend.txt's
# `rejectUnauthorized: false`, and it inherits their one known cost - a test
# dialing a self-signed httptest server trips it. That is a true positive the
# team may choose to accept, so mark those lines `// scaffold-allow`; most can
# instead drop the field and use httptest.NewTLSServer(h).Client().
#
# Only `: true` on a WHOLE-WORD field name fires. Silent: `InsecureSkipVerify:
# false`, `c.InsecureSkipVerify = insecure`, `if c.InsecureSkipVerify {`, and a
# longer identifier that merely ends with the name - `DisallowInsecureSkipVerify:
# true` means the OPPOSITE and used to fire before the left boundary was added.
#
# Comments are NOT silent. The hook is line-oriented, so commented-out code and
# godoc examples showing the field DO fire; mark them `// scaffold-allow`.
# Measured over 60,540 .go files - 48,866 of them third-party, across kubernetes,
# moby, cockroachdb, vault, terraform, etcd, grpc-go, hugo, minio, traefik,
# prometheus, golangci-lint and go-resty, plus the Go distribution: 595 lines
# name the field, 264 match, 261 are real bypasses and 3 are comment-only (two
# of those are go-resty godoc examples).
# A tried-and-rejected tightening - refusing to match after a `//` on the line -
# removed those 3 comments but ALSO silenced 3 real bypasses whose line carried a
# `"tcp://"` URL. Trading a blocked comment for a silent hole is a bad deal, so
# the rule stays line-blind and the marker is the escape hatch.
(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])InsecureSkipVerify[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*true	InsecureSkipVerify: true disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA, don't disable verification

# No shell-command rule ships here. An `exec.Command("sh", "-c", ...)` rule used
# to; it was measured over the corpus above and DROPPED. It fired on 24 lines,
# not one of them a defect a committer had to fix, and its own carve-out for
# fixed scripts only understood double-quoted literals, so moby's named constant
# `exec.Command("sh", "-c", nsCommand)` and a golangci-lint bench line whose
# script is a backtick raw string both fired. It is also defeated by plain gofmt:
# wrapping the call across lines hides the real sink from a line-oriented grep,
# so the shapes it did catch were the ones that happened to fit on one line.
# Use gosec's G204, which decides this on the AST with type information.

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])print[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin print() before committing (off by default: 'print' is a plausible method/field name)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])panic[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid panic() in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main/init and tests)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])log\.(Fatal|Panic)(f|ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid log.Fatal/log.Panic in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main)

# No ignored-error rule ships here, not even commented out. Go's `_` discards
# ordinary values as well as errors, and a line-oriented regex cannot tell
# `r0, _, _ := syscall.Syscall(...)`, `req, _ := http.NewRequest(...)` or the
# package-level `var _ = flag.Bool(...)` from a dropped error. The obvious
# pattern matched 21,185 lines in 6,076 of the 48,866 third-party .go files of
# the corpus above - 12% of every file - so even as an opt-in it is a trap. Use
# errcheck / golangci-lint, which decide this on the AST.
